{"id":"n-20261024-dllvu8","category":"health","display_title":"The Hidden Dangers of Fitness Addiction","display_title_he":"הסכנות הנסתרות של התמכרות לכושר","short_summary":"A Welsh runner shares his journey of overcoming a severe fitness addiction that caused anxiety and sleep deprivation. Experts highlight a growing trend of exercise obsession fueled by data and social media.","english_level":"C1","words":539,"reading_time":4,"image":null,"sources":[{"name":"BBC News","domain":"bbc.co.uk","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cmvg909xv1j8o","title":"Fitness became an addiction that left me anxious and sleep deprived","published_at":"2026-10-23T21:57:33.000Z"}],"published_at":"2026-10-23T21:57:33.000Z","first_published_in_app_at":"2026-10-24T06:03:40.952Z","quality_score":9,"interest_score":8,"verification_status":"verified","content_status":"published","paragraphs":["For many people, regular exercise represents a cornerstone of physical and mental well-being. However, experts warn that an excessive focus on training can sometimes transform into a harmful psychological condition. A striking case from Wales highlights how a passion for running can spiral into a debilitating obsession, impacting sleep, mental health, and personal life.","Wayne Davies, a forty-year-old runner residing in Newtown, Powys, initially started running a decade ago simply to maintain his physical health. Over time, his routine escalated dramatically from casual jogs to full marathons and grueling ultra-distance events, including a 120-kilometer race around the Llŷn Peninsula in Gwynedd. According to Davies, he became entirely consumed by training plans, race times, and digital fitness data, constantly comparing his achievements with other runners on social media. He admitted that he was never satisfied, perpetually chasing the next challenge while fearing failure, feeling as though the rug was being pulled from under his feet.","The situation intensified significantly after Davies experienced a frightening cardiac scare during a training run. Medical professionals diagnosed him with atrial fibrillation, a heart rhythm condition. Although treatment allowed him to resume running, the lingering fear of a recurrence heightened his obsessive behaviors. Eventually, the psychological toll left him chronically sleep-deprived, anxious, and struggling with concentration, affecting other areas of his life.","Recognizing that he needed intervention, Davies turned to his general practitioner with the encouragement of his family and friends. Although he felt a bit ashamed at first, believing men often see talking about feelings as a sign of weakness, he took that crucial step. He was subsequently signposted to the Powys-based charity Ponthafren and enrolled in a twelve-week online cognitive behavioral therapy program provided by NHS Wales, specifically the SilverCloud Space from Anxiety course. Through this structured support, he learned valuable strategies to manage his intrusive thoughts and let go of rigid expectations. Today, he no longer obsesses over daily fitness metrics, choosing instead to listen to his body and enjoy running for its own sake as he trains for Marathon Eryri.","Specialists in addiction and mental health point out that Davies is far from alone. Zaheen Ahmed, head of therapy at the UK Addiction Treatment Centre, reported a notable surge in demand for guidance regarding exercise compulsions, noting that more than 57,000 individuals have sought assistance through their exercise addiction platform in recent months. Ahmed explained that such fixations frequently emerge when individuals embark on sobriety and cross-addict into fitness, replacing substance dependencies with intense fitness regimes. Furthermore, modern applications and fitness trackers have added competitive elements that can inadvertently foster compulsive behavior disorders.","Professionals like Cardiff-based personal trainer Dafydd Judd emphasize that the boundaries between fitness routines and technology are increasingly blurring. Calorie counting and tracking apps can easily trigger obsessive tendencies if individuals become overly fixated on measurement and digital validation. Judd also warned that social media acts as a double-edged sword, noting that some people manipulate numbers to create false illusions of superiority, even though online communities can foster healthy competition. Fionnuala Clayton, project manager of NHS Wales' Online Cognitive Behavioural Therapy Service, expressed gratitude that Davies reached out during a challenging time, reminding everyone that accessible mental health support remains vital whenever a routine starts dominating everyday life."],"facts":[{"fact":"Wayne Davies, a 40-year-old runner from Newtown in Powys, became addicted to fitness, leading to anxiety and sleep deprivation.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"He progressed from casual jogs to marathons and a 75-mile ultra-distance race around the Llŷn Peninsula.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"His struggle worsened after a heart scare during training, resulting in an atrial fibrillation diagnosis.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"With the encouragement of family and friends, he sought help via his GP and completed a 12-week NHS Wales SilverCloud course.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Zaheen Ahmed from the UK Addiction Treatment Centre noted a rise in fitness addiction search traffic, with over 57,000 people seeking help recently.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Personal trainer Dafydd Judd warned that calorie counting and social media can contribute to obsessive fitness and tech behaviours.","sources":[1]}],"glossary":[{"text":"For","lemma":"for","pos":"prep","he":"עבור","name":false},{"text":"many","lemma":"many","pos":"adj","he":"רבים","name":false},{"text":"people","lemma":"people","pos":"noun","he":"אנשים","name":false},{"text":"regular","lemma":"regular","pos":"adj","he":"סדיר","name":false},{"text":"exercise","lemma":"exercise","pos":"noun","he":"פעילות גופנית","name":false},